      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The QRS axis was measured in 24 patients during ergonovine malate provocation test (EM test). Of 12 patients with significant spasm of the right coronary artery (RCA) and/or left circumflex artery (LCX), the QRS axis shifted to the right in 7 patients after the EM test (mean 8.2 degrees), and the axis shifted back to the left in 9 patients after nitroglycerin administration (mean -9.1 degrees). The sensitivity of right axis shift for RCA and LCX spasm was 58% and the specificity was 80%. Thus, right axis shift seems to be associated with myocardial ischemia due to RCA and LCX spasm and to be useful for the detection of RCA and/or LCX spasms."},
